---
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { EN } from "../data/copy";
---

<Legal
  copy={EN}
  title="Trademark policy"
  description="How you may use the Spark name and logo, and what a fork must change."
  path="/trademark/"
  altPath="/fr/marque/"
  altLang="fr"
>
  <p>
    The MIT licence covers the code. The Spark name, the wordmark and the symbol are not part of
    that grant. This page is the short version of the rule: keep the mark pointing at this
    project, and a fork keeps its own identity.
  </p>

  <h2>You may, without asking</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Refer to Spark by name, link to this site or the repository, and write about it.</li>
    <li>Say that your project is “built on Spark”, “works with Spark” or “compatible with Spark”, as long as it is true.</li>
    <li>Use screenshots of the app in articles, reviews, documentation and talks.</li>
    <li>Fork the code, modify it and run or share it privately.</li>
    <li>Keep the logo inside an unmodified copy of the app, exactly as distributed here.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>You may not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Present a fork, a build or a service as the official Spark, or imply that it is endorsed by this project.</li>
    <li>Use the Spark name or logo as the icon, name or branding of your own application.</li>
    <li>Use the mark in a way that suggests a certification, an audit or a guarantee that does not exist.</li>
    <li>Register the name or a confusingly similar mark as a trademark, a domain or a package name.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If you distribute a fork</h2>
  <p>
    Pick another name and another icon, and say clearly what it is based on — for example
    “Acme, a fork of Spark”. The MIT licence already requires you to keep the copyright notice;
    this is the same idea applied to the name.
  </p>

  <h2>No certified seal</h2>
  <p>
    Spark checks the SHA-256 of a package archive before extracting it. It does not verify a
    signature, and it does not audit the code of a community extension. Nothing on this site, and
    no badge in the app, should be read as “certified”. A community package is code you chose to
    trust.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    Open an issue on the <a href="https://github.com/nnnnnzo/sparkawe/issues" rel="noreferrer">repository</a> and
    describe the use. Small, non-commercial references are almost always fine.
  </p>
</Legal>
